    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Dengue virus (DENV) is a mosquito-transmitted virus that infects tens of millions worldwide each year. One currently underexplored behavior of DENV is its ability to regulate the innate immune system. Previous research has shown that DENV activates pathways associated with autophagy, complement, and apoptosis, and can inhibit these pathways in order to prevent antiviral responses. My preliminary investigation showed that monocytes significantly upregulate Nectin-2 following infection with DENV. Nectin-2 is a ligand for DNAX Accessory Molecule-1 (DNAM-1 or CD226), an important activating receptor on natural killer (NK) cells. Due to this upregulation, we hypothesized that Nectin-2 might play a role as an important recognition molecule for NK cells in DENV infection. After a series of optimization experiments, we conducted several tests co-culturing NK cells with DENV-infected target cells, using monoclonal antibodies to inhibit the Nectin-2/DNAM-1 pathway. While our study was terminated prematurely due to the COVID-19 outbreak, we observed evidence that the Nectin- 2/DNAM-1 pathway may have a modest effect on NK directed killing of DENV-infected cells. Further study will reveal whether these results are significant, or whether Nectin-2 may be an indicator of a different biological process.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
